828e3010-9758-47aa-8914-091e7df5b203	a5fc118d-f58c-465a-871b-a37acafaa21f	Tulips are spring-blooming perennials that grow from bulbs and are popular for their wide variety of colorful, cup-shaped flowers. Originally from Central Asia, they have thousands of cultivars and are often associated with the Netherlands, where they became a major part of the economy. To grow them, plant bulbs in autumn in well-drained soil, and they will bloom in the spring, requiring a cold period to rebloom each year.  \nCharacteristics and history\nAppearance: Tulips have a wide range of colors (though not true blue), sizes, and shapes, including single, double, and ruffled varieties. They typically have two to six bluish-green leaves at the base of the stem. \nOrigin: The name "tulip" comes from the Persian word for "turban" because of the flower's shape. They originated in Central Asia and were cultivated in Turkey before being brought to Europe in the 1500s. \nSymbolism: Tulips can symbolize different things depending on their color; for example, yellow is often linked to cheerful thoughts, while orange can represent enthusiasm. 	\N	[]	1	\N	\N	\N	0	0	{English}	\N	25	{}	f	f	0.00	0	\N	0.00	f	1	\N	2025-10-16 23:33:19.404+00	2025-10-16 23:54:38.282419+00	\N	\N	\N	0	0	f	\N	\N	\N	\N	{}	f	f	{https://ldxscjxoakqrmkgqwwhr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/photographer-portfolios/a5fc118d-f58c-465a-871b-a37acafaa21f/1760658831567-s6xwmx5og.jpg,https://ldxscjxoakqrmkgqwwhr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/photographer-portfolios/a5fc118d-f58c-465a-871b-a37acafaa21f/1760658831567-m8xps9mzq.jpg,https://ldxscjxoakqrmkgqwwhr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/photographer-portfolios/a5fc118d-f58c-465a-871b-a37acafaa21f/1760658831567-1giennf9i.jpg,https://ldxscjxoakqrmkgqwwhr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/photographer-portfolios/a5fc118d-f58c-465a-871b-a37acafaa21f/1760658831567-ps5ofuekv.jpg,https://ldxscjxoakqrmkgqwwhr.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/photographer-portfolios/a5f